{"term":"срам","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"sram","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sexual_shame","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":25,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Moral shame or sexual disgrace; in Orthodox usage also a euphemism for the genitals ('shameful parts').","english_term":"shame","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Derives from Proto-Slavic *sormŭ; in Church Slavonic and Orthodox homiletics. Phrase 'срамные места' = genitals."}
{"term":"срамота","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"sramota","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sexual_shame","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":79,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A disgraceful or shameful state of affairs, especially in sexual or moral matters; used exclamatorially to express moral outrage.","english_term":"disgrace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Nominalised intensification of срам; exclamatory use predominates in folk and colloquial speech ('какая срамота!')."}
{"term":"честь","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"chest'","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"female_chastity","concept":"female_sexual_honor","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Honor; in sexual contexts specifically a woman's chastity and sexual reputation as a socially enforced patriarchal and Orthodox moral value.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Primary Russian word for honor in all senses; in sexual discourse means female chastity as a family-owned social commodity. Loss of честь is a central plot driver in 19th-century Russian literature."}
{"term":"бесчестье","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"beschest'ye","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_dishonor","concept":"female_sexual_dishonor","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":5,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Dishonor; in sexual contexts the formal social category of a woman having lost her chastity and consequently her standing in community and family.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Antonym of честь; a grave social and legal category in pre-Soviet Orthodox Russia. The concept frames sexual dishonor as an irreversible status change, not merely a reputation problem."}
{"term":"позор","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"pozor","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"public_shaming","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Public disgrace or scandal; in sexual contexts the public humiliation of a person, especially a woman, for violating sexual norms.","english_term":"disgrace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"General-purpose word for shame/disgrace, not inherently sexual; mapped to slut_shaming here because in the honor-shame economy it functions as the mechanism of social enforcement against sexual transgressors."}
{"term":"опозорить","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"opozorit'","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"public_shaming","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To publicly disgrace or shame someone, typically by exposing sexual misconduct or destroying their reputation.","english_term":"to disgrace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Perfective verb (single completed act of disgracing); can apply to any public dishonoring but is frequently used in sexual honor contexts."}
{"term":"опозориться","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"opozorit'sya","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"self_disgrace","concept":"sexual_misbehavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To disgrace oneself; to bring shame upon oneself through illicit or sexually indecent behavior.","english_term":"to disgrace oneself","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Reflexive counterpart of опозорить; the self-directed version of public sexual shaming; implies the subject's own conduct caused the disgrace."}
{"term":"запятнать честь","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"zapyatnat' chest'","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"honor_taint","concept":"ravishment","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":12,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To stain or taint someone's honor; to destroy a woman's sexual reputation through seduction, rape, or public revelation of sexual misconduct.","english_term":"to taint one's honor","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Metaphor of staining/sullying fabric; 'запятнать' = to spot, to blot; used in 19th-century literature and folk speech for sexual violation or scandal that destroys female honor."}
{"term":"шлюха","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"shlyukha","category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_insult","concept":"slut_shame_woman","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":92,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Slut, whore; the primary Russian crude insult for a sexually promiscuous woman.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the most common sexual insults in Russian; used as a serious character attack and as casual hyperbole. Etymology uncertain; possibly from Polish 'szluja'. Near-mat register."}
{"term":"гулящая","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"gulyashchaya","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"promiscuous_woman","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":60,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":84,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A loose or promiscuous woman; literally 'the one who walks around (sexually)'; derived from гулять (to walk, to revel, to fool around).","english_term":"loose woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Traditional folk/colloquial pejorative; less offensive than шлюха but still stigmatizing. Also appears in historical and literary texts for women of easy virtue."}
{"term":"нагулять","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"nagulyat'","category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"promiscuous_sex","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":45,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To sleep around, to engage in promiscuous extramarital sex; literally 'to accumulate by walking/roving about.'","english_term":"to sleep around","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Prefix на- = accumulative result; гулять = to walk/revel/fool around. Used in phrases like 'нагулялась в молодости' (she slept around in her youth). Always implies extramarital context."}
{"term":"нагулять ребёнка","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"nagulyat' rebyonka","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"illegitimate_pregnancy","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":48,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":86,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To conceive and bear a child out of wedlock through promiscuous sexual behavior; literally 'to walk a child into existence.'","english_term":"to have an illegitimate child","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Extension of нагулять with direct object (ребёнка = child); always pejorative, attributing the pregnancy to the woman's promiscuity; the father is typically unnamed or unknown."}
{"term":"незаконнорождённый","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"nezakonnorozhdyonny","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"illegitimacy","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":5,"technical":78,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Illegitimate; born outside of marriage; the formal legal and bureaucratic term for a bastard in pre-Soviet Russian civil and church law.","english_term":"illegitimate","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound: незаконно (unlawfully) + рождённый (born); purely clinical/legal register with no inherent insult force, unlike байстрюк or бастард. Used in official documents and 19th-century literature."}
{"term":"байстрюк","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"baystryuk","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"illegitimacy","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":68,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":33,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":"UA","definition":"Bastard; a derogatory folk and dialectal term for an illegitimate child, used primarily in Ukrainian and South Russian speech.","english_term":"bastard","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily Ukrainian; enters Russian through Babel, Bulgakov, Southern dialects. Etymology disputed: possibly Polish 'bęckart' or Yiddish. Largely obsolete in contemporary standard Russian."}
{"term":"бастард","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"bastard","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"illegitimacy","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":52,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Bastard; an illegitimate child or general insult for a contemptible person; a direct loanword from French/English.","english_term":"bastard","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used in historical (noble genealogy, heraldry) and literary contexts, and also as a general modern insult. Less emotionally loaded in Russian than in English; slightly bookish."}
{"term":"падшая","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"padshaya","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"fallen_woman","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":22,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A fallen woman; a woman who has lost her virtue through sexual sin; used in Orthodox moral discourse and 19th-century Russian literature.","english_term":"fallen woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From пасть (to fall); strong Orthodox moral framing equating sexual sin with a spiritual fall from grace. Used by Dostoevsky, Tolstoy, et al. Not crude — literary and religiously charged. Russian equivalent of Victorian 'fallen woman'."}
{"term":"испорченная","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"isporchennaya","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"spoiled_woman","concept":"slut_shame_woman","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":42,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A spoiled or corrupted woman; one who is no longer sexually pure or virtuous; used as a pejorative for a sexually experienced or morally compromised woman.","english_term":"soiled woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'corrupted' or 'spoiled' (as food spoils); the sexual meaning is implicit and applied almost exclusively to women. The metaphor treats virginity as a commodity that degrades on contact."}
{"term":"потаскушка","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"potaskushka","category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_insult","concept":"slut_shame_woman","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A slut; crude colloquial insult for a sexually promiscuous woman who is 'dragged around' between sexual partners.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From потаскать/потащить (to drag around repeatedly); implies the woman has been physically passed from partner to partner. Diminutive suffix -ушка moderates form but not force."}
{"term":"блядство","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"blyadstvo","category":"insults","subcategory":"sexual_debauchery","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":88,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Whoring, gross sexual debauchery, promiscuity as a way of life; abstract noun derived from блядь (whore/slut); also used hyperbolically for any contemptible or dishonest conduct.","english_term":"whoring","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Near-mat word; broad extended meaning covers any morally reprehensible behavior beyond sexuality ('это политическое блядство'). The -ство suffix makes it an abstract/collective concept rather than a single act."}
{"term":"стыд","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"styd","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_shame","concept":"sexual_shame","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":5,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Shame; in the Russian sexual honor economy, the internalized negative affect that enforces sexual norms and polices individual behavior from within.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Distinguished from позор (external public disgrace) by being internal and self-directed. 'Как не стыдно' (have you no shame?) is a primary social sanction. The Russian honor-shame system treats стыд and позор as complementary enforcement mechanisms."}
{"term":"срамиться","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"sramit'sya","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"self_disgrace","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To shame oneself; to behave in a sexually or morally disgraceful manner; to make a public spectacle of one's own shameful conduct.","english_term":"to disgrace oneself","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Reflexive of срамить (to shame someone else); implies acting in a way that invites moral condemnation. More archaic than опозориться; primarily encountered in religious and folk literary contexts."}
{"term":"бесстыдница","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"besstydnitsa","category":"insults","subcategory":"shameless_woman","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A shameless woman; a woman who lacks sexual or moral shame and flaunts her behavior openly.","english_term":"shameless woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine noun; без-стыд-ница = without-shame-woman; less severe than шлюха; often used by older generations scolding younger women perceived as sexually immodest. Masculine form бесстыдник exists but is less charged."}
{"term":"обесчестить","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"obeschestit'","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"dishonoring","concept":"ravishment","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":18,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To dishonor someone; in historical and literary contexts specifically to rape or seduce a woman, thereby destroying her sexual honor and social standing irreversibly.","english_term":"to dishonor","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From обес-честить (to strip of honor); in 19th-century Russian literature typically a euphemism for rape or seduction-unto-ruin. Cf. usage in Pushkin, Dostoevsky, Ostrovsky. Modern usage more broadly means to disgrace in any context."}
{"term":"честь девушки","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"chest' devushki","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"female_chastity","concept":"female_sexual_honor","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A girl's honor; the specific set phrase denoting female virginity and sexual purity as a socially and morally valued patriarchal commodity in Russian culture.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Fixed phrase; 'защитить честь девушки' = to defend a girl's honor; 'лишить чести' = to take her honor. The concept frames female virginity as an asset belonging as much to the family as to the individual woman."}
{"term":"чичи","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"chichi","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":40,"recognition_pct_peak":48,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Female breasts; slang term used in Russian criminal argot (fenya) and some informal colloquial speech.","english_term":"tits","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Origin unclear; possibly reduplicative baby-talk (childish diminutive pattern) or borrowed via Romani into fenya. Restricted primarily to fenya speakers and users of urban criminal slang; low recognition outside that subculture."}
